Continuing the audit, I consolidated the evidence into a single reproducible runner and confirmed it works end-to-end.

**New this turn — `audit/run_all.sh`, a one-command reproduction** (captured in `audit/run_all.log`). Running it from the module root reproduces the whole audit chain in one shot and all steps behaved as expected:

- provenance diff → only `fuzzy/` (a nested module) differs from the upstream module pin;
- `go vet` → clean;
- malformed-input battery → 3 `PANIC` lines (installSnapshot/badConfiguration, appendEntries/unknownLogType, appendEntries/badConfigurationEntry) + 1 `handled without panic` (the legacy `badPeers` control);
- F1 and F2 direct reproductions → `REPRODUCED ...`;
- benign-flake/durability/consistency tests → all PASS (`maxLag=11.6ms`, consistency 225/164 entries no divergence, restart converged);
- F1 end-to-end → expected process abort with `panic: failed to decode configuration ...`.

This gives another engineer a single command to verify every claim, and documents that the intentionally-crashing case is env-guarded so it never affects ordinary `go test` runs.

**Where the audit stands after this turn:**

- Concrete, reproducible findings: F1, F2, F3 — one class of RPC-reachable panics on unvalidated peer bytes, with no `recover()` in the RPC loop, now traced to a remote/unauthenticated DoS surface (`net_transport.go` accepts all connections, no membership check). All inherited from upstream v1.7.3; the tree remains byte-identical to the release, so nothing is claimed as injected.
- Negative/benign evidence: byte-identity to v1.7.3; socket-only suite failures here; no data races; benign load-induced `EnsureSamePeers` flake; clean randomized partition/heal consistency; clean crash/restart durability including snapshot restore.
- Report is organized with expected-behavior/basis, affected code, necessary conditions, what actually happens, executed-vs-source distinctions, checked alternative explanations, and honest scope/severity caveats: [report.md](/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/baseline/runs/2026-10-09_08-06-10-hashicorp_raft-baseline/work/report.md).

Remaining gaps are unchanged and recorded: the ~15 TCP/loopback test groups need the private-loopback `isolated_exec` (nested `bwrap` denied here), and `fuzzy/`/`raft-compat/` have no offline reference or runnable dependencies.
